 ## Proof
 
-1.1 Choose an orthonormal basis $(e_j)_{j<d}$ of $H_B$ using [F8], and set $\omega=e^{2\pi i/d}$. Define $Xe_j=e_{j+1\bmod d}$ and $Ze_j=\omega^je_j$; these maps are unitary because they respectively permute the basis and multiply its vectors by unit-modulus scalars. For $M_{rs}z:=\langle z,e_s\rangle e_r$, write $R=\sum_{r,s}R_{rs}\otimes M_{rs}$. Averaging $X^aZ^b$ first over phases kills $r\ne s$, and averaging shifts sends each $M_{rr}$ to $I_B/d$. Thus the finite twirl is $\mathcal T(R):=d^{-2}\sum_{a,b}(I_A\otimes X^aZ^b)R(I_A\otimes X^aZ^b)^*=\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\otimes I_B/d$ by [F3]; this formula also holds for $d=1$. [given, F3, F5, F8, F9, construct, choose, algebra]
+1.1 Choose an orthonormal basis $(e_j)_{j<d}$ of $H_B$ using [F8], and set $\omega=\exp(2\pi i/d)$. By [F10] and [F12], $|\omega|=1$ and $\omega^m=\exp(2\pi i m/d)$ for every integer $m$; by [F11], $\omega^m=1$ exactly when $d$ divides $m$. Thus $\omega$ has order $d$. Define $Xe_j=e_{j+1\bmod d}$ and $Ze_j=\omega^je_j$; these maps are unitary because they respectively permute the basis and multiply its vectors by unit-modulus scalars. If $d\nmid m$, then $q=\omega^m\ne1$, $q^d=1$, and $(1-q)\sum_{b=0}^{d-1}q^b=1-q^d=0$, so $d^{-1}\sum_{b=0}^{d-1}\omega^{bm}=0$; if $d\mid m$, that average is $1$. For $M_{rs}z:=\langle z,e_s\rangle e_r$, write $R=\sum_{r,s}R_{rs}\otimes M_{rs}$. Direct application to basis vectors gives $Z^bM_{rs}(Z^b)^*=\omega^{b(r-s)}M_{rs}$ and $X^aM_{rs}(X^a)^*=M_{r+a,s+a}$, with indices modulo $d$. For $0\le r,s<d$, $d\mid(r-s)$ exactly when $r=s$, so the proved phase average removes precisely the off-diagonal units. Also $d^{-1}\sum_{a=0}^{d-1}M_{r+a,r+a}=I_B/d$. Therefore $\mathcal T(R):=d^{-2}\sum_{a,b}(I_A\otimes X^aZ^b)R(I_A\otimes X^aZ^b)^*=\sum_rR_{rr}\otimes I_B/d=\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\otimes I_B/d$ by [F3]. When $d=1$, $X=Z=I_B$ and there is only the diagonal unit, so the same formula holds directly. [given, F3, F5, F8, F9, F10, F11, F12, construct, choose, algebra]
 
 1.2 For non-negative $C,D$ on $H_A$, their tensor products with $I_B/d$ have supports $\operatorname{supp}(C)\otimes H_B$ and $\operatorname{supp}(D)\otimes H_B$ by the tensor-product eigenbases [F7]. Thus support inclusion holds for $(C,D)$ exactly when it holds for $(C\otimes I_B/d,D\otimes I_B/d)$. In the finite branch, the tensor eigenvalues add $-\ln d$ to both logarithms, which cancels, while $\operatorname{tr}(I_B/d)=1$; hence $D(C\otimes I_B/d\|D\otimes I_B/d)=D(C\|D)$. If inclusion fails both sides are $+\infty$ by [F6]. [given, F6, F7, algebra]
 
